Minh,

Yes two clients can write to disjoint areas of a file concurrently,
if the writes are not in the same "chunk" of the file.  DFS clients
obtain data tokens on "chunk" boundries.  The default chunk size
for a DFS client is 64K bytes.  The chunk size is configurable from
8K to 256K.  So for example if both clients are configured with
an 8K chunksize, then they can concurrently write to a file if
the writes are in different 8K regions of the file.  See the dfsd
man page for how to change the chunksize.

Carl Burnett

p.s.  I assume by concurrently you mean that the write by one client to
      a region of a file does not result in cache invalidation of the
      disjoint region at the second client. In other words no token revocation.

==================================================

