I heard this too, but as I understand it, the Common Desktop Environment (CDE) will
be enhanced via OSF PSTs.

: >----------------------------------------------------------------------------<
: |DCE  Big.  Making progress.
: >----------------------------------------------------------------------------<
:     This is correct...

Well, making progress is true.  However, DCE is under seige from various quarters,
including parts of some OSF sponsors.  MOMA is attempting to suggest that message
oriented middleware products collectively are some kind of alternative to DCE.
CORBA 2.0 repudiated DCE in favor of a new wire protocol, which hurts mind share
if nothing else. I have been told that the influential Meta Group is bad mouthing
DCE.  Vendors are still not offering bundled runtimes.  Noone is making any effort
to reach the small to medium ISVs (which are the main source of software inovation.)
The curve is upward, but the future is still uncertain.

: >----------------------------------------------------------------------------<
: |DME  I guess this is no longer an "official" OSF offering.  How do you
: |see it?
: >----------------------------------------------------------------------------<
:     I see DME as a non-existant technology that never got finished and that
: interested no one.

I disagree.  DME failed for reasons both under OSF's control and not under their
control.

First, definition.  DME consists of three relatively independent pieces:
Distributed Services (DS), Network Management Option (NMO) and Object Management
Framework (OMF).  DS and NMO have been finished and licensed.  OMF is on hold.  I
don't think anyone is selling the DS per se (the original technology providers
are selling the products that the DS were based on). I think the NMO is being sold.

The demand for NMO is small because it integrates CMIP and SNMP and (at least in
the US) the demand for CMIP is small.

The OMF was a failure because it is based on the premise that management requires
an object oriented approach (I agree), but object oriented standards are in their
infancy.

These things were not OSF's fault.  However, OSF did a very poor job of explaining
what DME was and what it was not.  In particular, the DS are really DCE applications
like DFS and should have been classified as such from the beginning.  People interested
in DME looked at them and said, I'm looking for management tools, not more DCE
infrastructure.  People interested in DCE never heard about the DS.

OSF also did a poor job of explaining the goals and limitations of the OMF. (partly
because they kept changing) Many people believed that DME would be the grand
integrating solution to all management problems.

: >----------------------------------------------------------------------------<
: |ANDF  Never adopted by anyone that I know of.  A good technical idea
: |that didn't attract any market?  What do you think?
: >----------------------------------------------------------------------------<
:     Your analysis sounds right to me...

ANDF is actually the most important technology to the Open Systems world.  Without
a binary distribution standard, open systems will remain what they are today, a
sideshow to the Intel/Microsoft world.  ANDF did not fail to attract a market.  It
was suppressed by the major system vendors, fearful of the ability of their ISVs
to sell on their competetor's platforms.  This shortsighted view reminds me of
the story about cooking a frog.  Put a frog in hot water and he jumps out. Put
him in cool water and turn up the heat and he gets cooked.  No one can use ANDF
until the vendors create "installers". Most users have not even heard of ANDF.  The
vendors will do nothing until they are forced, even though it is in their own
interest.  Why are "open systems proponents" like Mike Azzara of OST not demanding
ANDF?

Users should be demanding that their system vendors support it.  ANDF also needs 
to be enhanced to support a) relocatable libraries and b) language neutral objects.

(the above is a summary of a much longer economic argument)
 
: >----------------------------------------------------------------------------<
