I suggest your lecturers define what the requirements are, including
their budget (of timeframe, manhours, profitability [and how that is 
to be defined], etc), as to what an operating system should be.

In my mind, one of the best operating systems is a monitor (like the
old one on my CP/M machine).  It could run an operating system, editor,
assembler, application programs, batch files, etc in 4K ram (the monitor
took up 2K ROM, just because I made it big to have many features, this
was not required at all, or I could make a 16 byte rom boot the system
from floppy disks!)

Unix has its own elegance.  MS-DOS owes much of its architecture to 
many operating systems that came before it.  The same can be said for 
windose (yes I know, it is not an operating system, but a user interface
environment that enables GUI user interaction), OS/2, and many others.  Most
of them came from original batch oriented systems (the machines were so slow
that people didn't want to wait for them, so they went to lunch rather than
watch the machine grind), and interactive systems (not real-time but systems
that allowed bi-directional communications with people).

Actually, my wifes bread machine has a pretty good operating system.  It 
does perform real-time work, with both a batch (making the bread) and interactive
(responds and displays in response to buttons) components.  It fulfills a
need for human/machine interaction to allow useful work to get done.

Operating systems tend to be evolutionary in order, not revolutionary.

Let us know what your lecturers think! ... JC
