jlp@st-andrews.ac.uk (John Laing Page) wrote:

> I have lecturers who currently are using MS-DOS and UNIX
> as examples of badly designed software.

As opposed to what? Oberon? TRON? Other OSs beloved in academia, but
not ready for the prime time? The MacOS? Very sophisticated under the
hood, but wasted on the dummies that seem to be Apple's target
audience.

There is much to like in MS-DOS (especially all the bits that crept in
from Unix--I/O redirection, ability to use something other than
CP/M-compatible COMMAND.COM, etc.) and much to hate (leftovers from
CP/M, failure to properly understand the Unix original, lack of serial
port support, allowing programmers to bypass the OS and talk directly
to the hardware, etc.), but what can one expect from something made by
humans?

> Whilst I agree in part with this I think the MS-DOS was fine for the
> original hardware it was written to run on, which could most likely
> not have supported anything larger.

"Quick and dirty port of CP/M" would be more descriptive.

> UNIX, well thats what happens when things are designed by comittee.

Huh? Unix was originally developed by a small handful of men working
at close quarters. One problem was that they assumed that anyone using
it would be equally sophisticated. The committees came later, when
AT&T started commercializing Unix. One must also now forget the
generations of grad students hacking away at the BSD kernel.

On the whole, I would say the Unix is anarchy, not a committee
product. 

> However I have to prepare a document about operating system design
> and would value any comments about designing a new operating system.

> o Would it be worthwhile at the moment

Probably not. I once saw Unix described as the last great operating
system. It's getting too expensive to develop new operating systems
and, on the user side, too expensive to change over. Things like
Oberon and TRON just never reach critical mass--no matter how much
money the Swiss and Japanese governments throw at them.

> o What should it include

Or *not* include, I would say. The trend is toward microkernels and
systems that allow you to throw out facilities that you don't use.

> o Need it be backwardly compatible.

On what level? This is a complex issue because, in the usual "chicken
and egg" situation, the only way to build momentum is by attracting
users and the best way to do that is by giving them something close to
what they know. (I don't hear much about LISP machines these days.)

This starts at the lowest levels. Intel 80x86 assembler was child's
play for me since I'd been using 8080 assembler under CP/M. Further
back, 8080 assembler was obviously influenced by the IBM System 360,
the second computer system I used.

The next step up is C--in most respects, just a portable macro
assembler. If you're going to offer a C compiler, the first thing the
programmers want is the set ANSI standard C libraries, which, like it
or not, have a distinct Unix bias when it comes to assumptions as to
what sort of services should be provided by the system. I remember the
contortions that we went through under CP/M with BDS C since the
command line interpreter didn't support I/O redirection (which
predates Unix and C, BTW), for example.

> Also if anyone wants to support the case for MS-DOS and UNIX then
> feel free to do so.

They'll do until something better comes along. <g> Like most users,
I'm more concerned with the software than the underlying OS. (Windows
has more or less killed DOS, unfortunately. <sigh>)

