Hari,

The OSF DCE System Administration Guide says that when the "password
valid" flag is set to NO, "the system prompts the principal to change
the password" which is not to say that the system requires the password
be changed.  This prompt is no doubt the "Password must be changed!"
message you're seeing.  Elsewhere, the Guide says "Users must change
their passwords when they expire," but I don't think I'll be able to
change my password if I am dead.

Despite the verbiage in the Admin Guide, there does not appear to be
any way to get DCE to force a user to change the account password when
the password has expired.  Some vendors' integrated logins may do this,
but your observation holds at our site too: Setting an account
"password valid" flag to NO merely makes the "Password must be
changed!" message show up at every login.

You can however rely on the system to let you know when the passwords
are stale, and if you really want users to change their passwords, you
can get nasty with them.  You can scan the registry for accounts whose
passwords are expired (plus a few day perhaps), and change the password
on the account, or disable logins.  The users will have to get in touch
with you to re-establish the account.  They may be pretty angry
though.

I guess it depends on your policy and how badly you want passwords
changed regularly.

-Mark


