Hi,

Posting this for a friend and collaegue. Any hints for him can be
send to my mail address. I will forward and resume answers here if
there is enough interest.


Thank you.


Dominique





1. Introduction

This question list has been produced in order to get some information to 
help us define a DCE architecture for a commercial banking network.  
One also has to know that Encina from Transarc is 
layered on top of DCE to provide the OLTP functionality to the system.


2. Remote Procedure Calls

2.1 Is Privilege Delegation possible on DCE level 

Is it possible that a DCE server can call another service, using the 
privileges of its client ?  In other words, can the privileges of a 
client be exported to the server, to let the server use these privileges 
when accessing other servers ?
The reason why this question is asked, is to investigate the 
possibility of servers to access resources in such a way that access 
to these resources is controlled by the privileges of the user of the 
client application.	

+------+      +-------+       +-------+
|client|----->|server1|------>|server2|  and server1 accessing server2 
					 with the privileges of
+------+      +-------+       +-------+	 the client.

3. DCE Cell Directory Service

3.1 What to think about the following solutions (comment, please) ?
For this moment, the most popular design is the one with one cell per 
geographic region, including a gateway.  Is it possible to give PROs and CONs on each 
type of design.

1. One cell for whole system
2. One cell per region, 1 or more gateways to a mainframe included in the 
region cell  (6 cells in total)
3. One cell per region, gateways clustered in central cell 
(7 cells in total)
4. each (kind of) branch is a cell, gateways clustered in 
central cell: (1200 cells in total)

One also has to keep in mind the following rules :

A branch must be capable of operating off-line (up to 3 days).  
This autonomy is very important.

For CDS architecture one proposes to distribute the master replicas of 
resources across the cell.  More precisely locating master replicas in 
the branch containing the resources the replicas refer to.  Another 
proposal concerning the security service, proposes a master security 
server in the region HQ, while every branch has its own security replica 
server, to provide the autonomy needed when being off-line.  
Another advantage of this solution is the fact that a supplementary 
amount of security network traffic is avoided on the slow 64k WAN.  
One can resume that every branch has a replicated security server and a 
CDS server containing master replicas of the information concerning the 
branch itself and a minority of slave replicas of other branches 
(only from hierarchical lower positioned branches, info from higher 
positioned branches is cached by the CDS clerk).


4. The Global Directory Service (Name service)

4.1 GDS or DNS, which one to use ?

For inter-cell RPCs, which directory service is preferred, given that :
For the first five years (estimated), this kind of naming services will 
only be used to provide inter-cell access.
What system can give the most advantages (Comparing DNS with GDS, based on PROs and CONs) in terms of flexibility, international standards, and future integration.

4.2 What exactly is the extra load of  inter-cell requests, compared to 
the intra-cell RPC ?

This question is important for the decision about where to locate the 
gateways (each in one cell or all together in one central cell).  
Of course this also concerns remote positioning (one branch accessing 
another branch in the system, without the counter operator seeing 
any difference) between regions (cells).
Questions :
What is the additional effort required for inter-cell RPCs, compared 
to intra-cell RPCs ?
What is the additional security traffic involved with inter-cell RPCs ?
What restrictions are related to inter-cell authentication and 
authorization ?

5. Security

5.1 Is the security proposal a workable solution ?

The currently recommended proposal provides a cell as large as one zone.
This means that about 1600 users will populate one cell.  
Is it possible to have a registry per region 
containing the necessary principals  for 1600 users, 
and 200 UNIX servers with X-terminals. 
More important, can this registry be replicated to all branches, 
to provide maximum availability ?


6. Manageability

6.1 Given the fact that all base services are present in a branch, 
what are the advantages and limitations of defining a cell per branch ?


Resulting from the decisions in the preferred solutions on CDS and 
Security, one can ask the question why not defining a cell per branch, 
since all branches have their own (replicated) servers.  
(I can find some reasons, but I want your ideas about this, 
even if this might look a bit strange at first sight)

6.2 How recoverable are DCE services ?

Is it possible for a DCE system to recover from an incidental shutdown ?
Can a DCE service be restarted after failure ?
These questions mostly concern the ability of DCE servers to restart 
autonomously from any possible system failure.  
This is important for the availability of the branch system and the 
load on the system administration.  Server replication and high-available
back-up systems are NO VIABLE SOLUTIONS !!  This question concerns the 
restart of DCE services when a branch is off-line (The bank has had these
line quality problems for years, and depends completely on the national 
telecom service) and other serves are not accessable.  
It also may be clear that a clustered system in every branch is far 
too expensive !

6.3 Numbers ?

We would like to find some numbers on the operation of DCE :
Question									|RAM	|Disk

What is the amount of system memory required to run Sec, DCE and DTS Clients 	|	|
What is the amount of system memory required to run the Security server  	|	|
What is the amount of traffic between CDS servers besides updates 		|	|
What is the amount of traffic increase while using authenticated RPCs 		|	|
What is the amount of traffic increase for each level of security 		|	|
(compared to no security)
What is the average amount of memory used per Registry principal 		|	|
What is the average amount of memory used per CDS entry				|	|

What is the traffic generated by one CDS update per replica 
What is the traffic generated by one registry update per security 
replica 

6.4 What is the (integration) possibility for DCE (system) management 
tools ?

6.5 Can DCE administration be integrated in the HP openView framework ?  
Are there any third party tools available to manage (parts of) the 
DCE environment ?

6.6 Are there any hardware and/or UNIX (kernel, devices, software to 
enable installation) requirements for DCE to run on the HP-UX machines ?

6.7 What are the implications of DCE version upgrades ?

What administrative work will have to be done when an upgrade of 
DCE will be executed on the unix servers ?  Which servers 
will have to be reconfigured and/or restarted ?
How easily can a branch with the proposed architecture get changed 
from one cell to another ?
Changing the cell name can always be a difficult task.  Are there any 
means to make the change of a part of a cell (a branch) to another cell 
easier (The configuration will remain the same, but the contents of 
the databases, managed by the different DCE servers, will change).

6.8 How to backup DCE services

What are the requirements to backup the data managed by the DCE 
services. (All servers down, no client request ?)  What must be backed 
up ?



Thank you for any suggestions you may have.


