In <D0rJCz.GI8@Belgium.EU.net> dierick@eunet.be (Dominique Dierick) writes:
>2.1 Is Privilege Delegation possible on DCE level 
>Is it possible that a DCE server can call another service, using the 
>privileges of its client ?

Starting with DCE 1.1, which OSF just released a couple of weeks ago, yes.

>3. DCE Cell Directory Service
[detailed question about cell design.]

I'm not qualified to comment -- I haven't set up any real cells.
OSF consulting services might be able to help, but they will charge
money (that's what consultants do :-) -- email to Kristin Knotts, kjk@osf.org,
for more info.

>4.1 GDS or DNS, which one to use ?

If you are not already running X.500 everywhere, then you will probably
find it much easier to use DNS-style names.

>4.2 What exactly is the extra load of  inter-cell requests, compared to 
>the intra-cell RPC ?

A client in cell A does an rpc_ns_import of a name for something in
cell B.  The name is parsed and the name for cell B is "pulled out."
Then the GDA either does an X.500 lookup or a DNS query to find out
where a CDS server can be found in cell B.  (That info is cached.)
Then the cell A code hands the rest of the name over to cell B's CDS
to resolve.

Once the client has the rpc_ns_import result, then it just does its
RPC's.  Authenticated RPC has a bit extra cost, detailed below.

>What is the additional effort required for inter-cell RPCs, compared 
>to intra-cell RPCs ?

Explained above.

>What is the additional security traffic involved with inter-cell RPCs ?

I forget the details (it's too late at night right now) but the secd's
in each cell get involved so that essentially one "extra" authenticated
RPC is done.

>What restrictions are related to inter-cell authentication and 
>authorization ?

The only DCE limitation right now is that a group created in the
secd of cell A cannot have principals who are in the secd in cell B.
I.e., groups cannot have foreign principals.


>5.1 Is the security proposal a workable solution ?

Again, I don't know.  The cell, however, is defined not just by CDS
but (more importantly in a way) by secd.  A secd defines what a cell is.

>6.1 Given the fact that all base services are present in a branch, 
>what are the advantages and limitations of defining a cell per branch ?

I don't know.

>Is it possible for a DCE system to recover from an incidental shutdown ?
>Can a DCE service be restarted after failure ?

Generally, yes.  In this regard DCE is not particularly different from
other kinds of servers.  If the disk is trashed, you need backups.
If the host is trashed, then it would help to have replicas and turn
those replicas into new masters.


As for the rest of your questions, sorry.  I got tired.
	/r$


